WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.027 --> 00:00:03.189
- I started painting music just

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:04.747
because I would try to explain

00:00:04.747 --> 00:00:08.032
synesthesia so many
times, it never quite made

00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:10.837
as much sense to describe it verbally,

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:12.224
so I thought it would be best just

00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:14.571
to put it on canvas just
because I've always been

00:00:14.571 --> 00:00:16.896
an artist and it made it much easier

00:00:16.896 --> 00:00:20.146
for people to be able to understand it.

00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:24.875
My name is Melissa McCracken, and I am

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:26.625
a synesthetic artist.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.070
Synesthesia is a neurological condition

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.310
where your brain is basically cross-wired,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.049
so certain stimuli will come in

00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:36.822
and it'll create the wrong
response in my brain.

00:00:36.822 --> 00:00:39.286
So for me that's listening to music

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:42.786
and it's translated into color in my head.

00:00:48.587 --> 00:00:51.478
I'm going to paint
Superstition by Stevie Wonder.

00:00:51.478 --> 00:00:53.430
I love the song Superstition just because

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.027
it's dynamic and funky and
just fun and expressive.

00:00:58.027 --> 00:00:59.627
Whenever I start a piece, I have to listen

00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:01.643
to the song first to even know what kind

00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:03.606
of colors I'd like to use.

00:01:03.606 --> 00:01:05.483
There are some songs that I hate

00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:07.894
that I like the way that
they look a little bit.

00:01:07.894 --> 00:01:10.955
Like a lot of pop music
can be pink and purple

00:01:10.955 --> 00:01:14.102
and, you know just all
these fun bright colors

00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:16.470
but the song's not that good.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.638
So one or the other, I
appreciate it somehow.

00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:20.982
You know you kind of have to really be

00:01:20.982 --> 00:01:23.702
in the right vibe to be able to paint.

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:24.647
If you're not in the right vibe,

00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:27.647
you're not gonna make anything good.

00:01:31.261 --> 00:01:34.738
I think the prettiest genre
of music is jazz music.

00:01:34.738 --> 00:01:37.341
I love blues and golds and whites,

00:01:37.341 --> 00:01:41.447
and it just seems very pearly
and iridescent a little bit.

00:01:41.447 --> 00:01:43.634
When I listen to Etta James’ At Last,

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:45.074
the thing that stuck
out most to me was just

00:01:45.074 --> 00:01:46.855
her voice at the very beginning

00:01:46.855 --> 00:01:49.682
when she goes into the "At last..."

00:01:49.682 --> 00:01:51.849
♫ At last

00:01:53.629 --> 00:01:57.671
And it's just a very bright
and but also warm sort

00:01:57.671 --> 00:02:00.914
of feeling and very kind of classic jazzy,

00:02:00.914 --> 00:02:04.263
which jazz music generally has a very gold

00:02:04.263 --> 00:02:06.596
and blue sort of look to it.

00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:13.181
Music has always been a
very big part of my life.

00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:15.207
My older brother is very musical

00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:17.330
and when I think back of him playing

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.431
the guitar for me, I think of the colors

00:02:19.431 --> 00:02:21.681
of what those memories are.

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:23.879
I was always a little disappointed

00:02:23.879 --> 00:02:26.717
because I was never
very musically inclined.

00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:29.639
It was really cool to
have a way to bring music

00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:33.722
into, you know, my life in
a different sort of way.

